WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.860
SHARLENE CHIU:
I'm Sharlene Chiu.

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:05.280
And I'd like you to imagine
you're the mayor of Boston.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.700
So let's say you want to reduce
energy consumption in the city.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.170
That's a good goal to have.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:12.420
So you're thinking about
passing different policies,

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.850
like reducing the number
of private vehicles

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:16.480
in the city center.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.220
Now, thanks to this simulation
platform that my lab built,

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.890
you can actually take
your policy ideas

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:26.400
and simulate them in a
virtual city of Boston.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:30.220
Now, even though you have this,
you have two more problems.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:33.370
First is Boston could
change in 50 years.

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:35.410
The population could go down.

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:36.720
The fuel price could go up.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.310
With all these
uncertainty factors,

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:41.370
you actually have way too many
possible futures scenarios

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.380
that Boston could enter.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.240
And we just can't
simulate all of them.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:46.410
It's too slow.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:50.040
So I come in, and I
developed a technique

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:54.040
to help you figure out which
scenarios you want to test.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:57.630
So my technique minimizes the
number of scenarios you need,

00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:01.110
while also making sure that
you have enough scenarios

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:03.930
to represent the whole
possibility space.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.550
So once you have
these scenarios,

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:09.000
you can take your
policies, your scenarios,

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.550
and stuff them into
the simulation.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:14.730
And then you get numbers
out-- energy consumption.

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:16.890
And so you can actually
take these numbers

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:20.240
and see which policies
failed in which scenarios

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.560
and succeeded in
other scenarios.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.210
But you'd like to know why they
failed and why they succeeded.

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.790
And so my second
part of the project

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:32.490
is helping you figure
out, what do scenarios

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:35.010
look like when a policy fails?

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:38.340
In doing so, you can
actually zoom in and say, OK,

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:41.640
as long as Boston avoids these
scenarios, you're good to go.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:43.380
Or you can say,
let's actually dig

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:48.210
in to why everything fails when
population and ridesharing go

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:48.720
down.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:49.980
Let's investigate that.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.270
You have that option now.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:54.090
And so at the end of
the day, my research

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:56.130
gave you a tool
to figure out what

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:58.789
to put into the
simulation, and a tool

00:01:58.789 --> 00:02:01.080
to figure out what to do with
the numbers that come out

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:02.490
of the simulation.

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.890
And hopefully, that
gives you, the mayor,

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:08.419
a way to create better informed
decisions on which policies

00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:10.189
you should pass.

